## 3. Main Issue Investigated --- MultiScanner Logging

The tester Journal repeatedly showed:

``` text
MultiScanner: skipped (MaxShortTrades=0, Phase 2 BUY-only mode)
```

The relevant code was:

``` cpp
if(MaxShortTrades == 0)
{
   Print("MultiScanner: skipped (MaxShortTrades=0, Phase 2 BUY-only mode)");
}
else
{
   static bool s_multiScannerInited = false;
   static string s_scanSymbols[] = {"BTCUSD", "EURUSD", "GBPUSD"};
   static int s_scanMagic[] = {1050, 1051, 1052};
   static int s_scanAtrHandle[] = {-1, -1, -1};
   static int s_scanEma9Handle[] = {-1, -1, -1};
   static int s_scanEma21Handle[] = {-1, -1, -1};
   static int s_scanRsiHandle[] = {-1, -1, -1};
   static datetime s_lastScanTime = 0;
}
```

There is only one source-code line producing this particular message,
but if the code is reached on every `OnTick()` while
`MaxShortTrades == 0`, that one `Print()` can execute repeatedly across
a very large number of simulated ticks.

A safer performance pattern is to log the condition only once:

``` cpp
static bool multiScannerSkipLogged = false;

if(MaxShortTrades == 0)
{
   if(!multiScannerSkipLogged)
   {
      Print("MultiScanner disabled: MaxShortTrades=0, Phase 2 BUY-only mode");
      multiScannerSkipLogged = true;
   }
}
else
{
   multiScannerSkipLogged = false;

   // Existing MultiScanner code
}
```

This does not change trading behavior; it only reduces repeated
diagnostics.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4. Result After the MultiScanner Investigation

The same historical range was tested again.

The runtime remained approximately in the same range.

This is an important finding:

> The repeated MultiScanner `Print()` is not sufficient to explain the
> overall very slow Strategy Tester runtime.

Also, because `MaxShortTrades == 0`, the main MultiScanner `else` branch
is skipped. Therefore the BTCUSD/EURUSD/GBPUSD scanner calculations are
not currently proven to be the main source of the delay.

